WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.080
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 81 for June 23rd, 2026.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.620
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:16.980
Let's go. Picture this. You only finished a chapter in your textbook or an article for a project.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.040
Instead of asking an AI to summarize everything for you, you try something different.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:28.160
You read it first yourself, then use the AI like a study partner that quizzes you on the parts

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:34.240
that still feel fuzzy. That idea is blowing up on Reddit right now, and it flips how most people

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:40.480
think about tools like chat GPT. Think of it like training for a sport. If someone else runs

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.460
the laps for you, your muscles never get stronger. But if you run the laps and then ask a coach

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:52.320
to point out where your form slipped, you actually improve. The same thing happens with learning.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.380
When you read first and then ask the AI to create a short quiz, your brain does the heavy

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:04.420
lifting. You stay in charge instead of letting the tool take over. For example, after finishing

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:11.240
a section, you could paste a paragraph into chat GPT and type a simple request. Ask it to explain

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.400
the tricky sentences like you are brand new to the topic. Then follow up by saying create three

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:23.040
multiple choice questions about the main ideas. You answer those questions yourself, check the

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:29.280
results, and notice exactly what stuck and what did not. Another angle is writing your own quick

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.520
summary of what you read and asking the AI to check it for accuracy. It can point out missing

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:40.320
details without rewriting the whole thing for you. This approach matters because it turns the AI

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.360
into a memory gym rather than a shortcut that leaves you with nothing. Students prepping for exams

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:53.300
or anyone trying to understand dense material can use it right now for free. Open chat GPT or any

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:58.960
similar chat bot on your phone, paste a short section from something you are reading and try that

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.580
quiz request yourself. You will feel the difference when you have to recall the ideas without the AI

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:10.640
handing them over. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show. We are going to look under the hood

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.720
at how these tools actually spot patterns in the first place. You know how when you glance at a

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.920
photo of a cat, your brain instantly picks up the pointy ears and whiskers without you thinking step

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:27.480
by step. Imagine building a system that copies that process but works only with numbers. It chops

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:32.840
the photo into tiny pieces, checks patterns like edges and colors across millions of examples,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:39.760
and slowly learns which combinations usually mean cat versus dog. Picture stacking many of these

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.200
pattern checking layers on top of each other. The first layer spot simple lines, the next layer

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.860
combines them into shapes like ears or eyes, and deeper layers recognize the whole animal.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:58.060
Each layer passes its findings to the next one, getting more specific as a ghost until the final

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:03.800
layer can name what the image shows, and that is basically what a neural network is doing when it

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:10.240
recognizes objects and pictures or predicts the next word in a sentence. It is not magic or actual

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:16.440
thinking. It is just lots of simple pattern matching steps chained together and trained on huge

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:22.240
amounts of data. So next time someone says neural network, you can tell them it is basically a

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:28.120
digital version of how your brain spots familiar patterns just done with math instead of biology.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:34.940
Not so scary, right? One of the coolest experiments I have seen lately is a snake game that slowly

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:41.400
figures out it is trapped inside a video game. An AI called Fable started with a simple prompt

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.940
to build the classic snake game. Then it kept improving the game across many rounds without

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:52.920
needing new instructions each time. The result smoothly switches between styles like real-time

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:59.000
strategy or farming sims while you still control the snake. Think of it like a band that starts playing

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.460
one song and then decides mid-performance to blend in a completely different genre without missing a

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:12.220
beat. You can play the finished version right now in any browser at snakestablebuild.netlify.app.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.900
Try moving the snake around and watch for the moments when the game starts hinting that the snake

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:24.360
knows it is in a simulation. It is a fun no sign-up experiment that shows how far AI game building

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:31.780
has come. A Reddit post compares an AI-generated image from October 2023 with one made in June

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:39.260
2026 using the same basic idea. The difference in detail, lighting and realism is striking between

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:45.560
those two outputs. It is a quick visual reminder of how fast these tools are improving for anyone who

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:51.660
likes creating art or just messing around with prompts. The comparison was shared on the chat GPT

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:58.880
subreddit. The October 2023 version and the June 2026 version were produced from identical starting

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:05.560
prompts. Viewers can see the gains and sharpness and shading side by side. The post was linked at

